YUME daemon for Debian
======================

The yume-daemon package installs yumed, /etc/yume/yumed.json, seed
/etc/yume/authorized_keys and /etc/yume/auth_keys.meta files, and a hardened
systemd unit named yumed.service.

The service is intentionally disabled and not started on package install.
Before enabling it, provide:

  * /etc/yume/server.crt
  * /etc/yume/server.key
  * at least one client public key in /etc/yume/authorized_keys
  * optional per-key permissions in /etc/yume/auth_keys.meta

The daemon runs as the locked yume user. Keep private key material readable
only by root and the yume group, for example:

  chown root:yume /etc/yume/server.key
  chmod 0640 /etc/yume/server.key

Then check the installed binary and start the service:

  yumed --config /etc/yume/yumed.json --version
  systemctl start yumed.service

The unit grants CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE so yumed can bind TCP port 443 without
running as root. It reads /etc/yume but writes only its runtime, state, and log
directories. Use TCP passthrough if a reverse proxy or load balancer sits in
front of yumed; do not terminate TLS in HTTP mode.
